== 4.9 Chapter 4 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [The narrator of a novel is best described as],
  [always a character in the story],
  [the author under another name],
  [a voice constructed by the author to tell the story],
  [the reader's representative]
)

#mcq(2, [A first-person narrator],
  [knows everything in the book],
  [never appears as a character],
  [must be the author],
  [reports only what the “I” has seen, heard or guessed]
)

#mcq(3, [A narrator who enters the minds of several characters is],
  [omniscient],
  [limited],
  [unreliable],
  [objective]
)

#mcq(4, [Third-person objective narration reports],
  [the author's opinions],
  [every character's thoughts],
  [only speech and visible action],
  [one character's thoughts]
)

#mcq(5, [“She would not go. She had never gone, and she would not begin now.” The technique is],
  [epistolary narration],
  [objective narration],
  [free indirect style],
  [first person]
)

#mcq(6, [A narrator is unreliable when],
  [he knows too little about the furniture],
  [he uses long words],
  [his account contradicts itself or is corrected by events],
  [he is a character]
)

#mcq(7, [The chief advantage of first-person narration is],
  [objectivity],
  [intimacy and the possibility of suspicion],
  [a wide social panorama],
  [complete information]
)

#mcq(8, [The chief advantage of omniscient narration is],
  [mystery],
  [the absence of a narrator],
  [immediacy],
  [the ability to compare minds and move across time and space]
)

#mcq(9, [A character built from a single trait, who cannot surprise the reader, is],
  [a foil],
  [dynamic],
  [flat],
  [round]
)

#mcq(10, [A character who changes during the novel is],
  [flat],
  [round],
  [static],
  [dynamic]
)

#mcq(11, [A foil is a character who],
  [is placed beside another to make the difference visible],
  [appears only once],
  [changes the plot],
  [narrates the story]
)

#mcq(12, [Characterisation through “others' reports” must be read with care because],
  [reports are always false],
  [characters cannot speak],
  [reports are not evidence],
  [the reporter's bias shapes the portrait]
)

#mcq(13, [“A most generous man,” writes the narrator, having just shown the man counting the spoons. The comment is],
  [irrelevant],
  [ironic, and the reader is invited to reverse it],
  [sincere praise],
  [a stage direction]
)

#mcq(14, [Showing differs from telling in that showing],
  [presents action and speech and lets the reader conclude],
  [states the conclusion],
  [is always better],
  [uses longer words]
)

#mcq(15, [Setting in a novel is best analysed as],
  [a set of pressures on the characters],
  [the author's birthplace],
  [geography],
  [the time of composition]
)

#mcq(16, [Atmosphere in prose refers to],
  [the historical period],
  [the feeling produced by the setting and the writing],
  [the narrator's reliability],
  [the weather]
)

#mcq(17, [A novel that opens in the middle of an action begins],
  [circularly],
  [episodically],
  [in medias res],
  [with a frame narrative]
)

#mcq(18, [A story told inside another story uses],
  [a flashback only],
  [free indirect style],
  [an episodic structure],
  [a frame narrative]
)

#mcq(19, [An episodic plot is characterised by],
  [the absence of characters],
  [loosely linked episodes, often on a journey],
  [a return to the opening],
  [a single tight action]
)

#mcq(20, [A circular structure produces the effect that],
  [time runs backwards],
  [the world is unchanged even if the character is not],
  [the narrator is unreliable],
  [the plot is unresolved]
)

#mcq(21, [The most important structural question to ask of a chapter is],
  [where it begins, what it withholds and where it ends],
  [whether it uses dialogue],
  [how many characters appear],
  [how long it is]
)

#mcq(22, [A motif differs from a symbol in that a motif],
  [has a fixed single meaning],
  [is always visual],
  [belongs only to poetry],
  [works by repetition, revising its meaning each time]
)

#mcq(23, [A novel of formation, following a character from childhood to maturity, is a],
  [bildungsroman],
  [novella],
  [fable],
  [picaresque]
)

#mcq(24, [A novel told entirely in letters is],
  [allegorical],
  [epistolary],
  [picaresque],
  [satirical]
)

#mcq(25, [An allegory differs from a symbol in that allegory],
  [uses one image once],
  [is systematic, with figures standing for ideas throughout],
  [is always comic],
  [has no plot]
)

#mcq(26, [A fable is distinguished by],
  [its use of verse],
  [a stated moral, often with animal characters],
  [its length alone],
  [its historical accuracy]
)

#mcq(27, [Magical realism is marked by],
  [explaining its magic carefully],
  [avoiding description],
  [being set in the future],
  [reporting the impossible as ordinary and unexplained]
)

#mcq(28, [Biography and autobiography belong to the prose genre because],
  [they are true],
  [they use the same narrative machinery — voice, point of view, structure, style],
  [they are long],
  [they contain dialogue]
)

#mcq(29, [Diction in prose refers to],
  [the arrangement of chapters],
  [the point of view],
  [the choice of words],
  [the length of sentences]
)

#mcq(30, [A paragraph of eleven very short sentences describing a fire most likely produces],
  [confusion],
  [formality],
  [speed and shock],
  [humour]
)

#mcq(31, [“The description appeals to the senses” is a weak answer because],
  [descriptions never do],
  [it is factually wrong],
  [it does not say which sense, in which phrase, or to what effect],
  [it concerns poetry only]
)

#mcq(32, [Summary is used in fiction chiefly to],
  [compress time and pass over what need not be lived through],
  [replace dialogue],
  [confuse the chronology],
  [bore the reader]
)

#mcq(33, [A narratee is],
  [the reader in the shop],
  [the person to whom a narrator addresses the tale],
  [the author's editor],
  [a flat character]
)

#mcq(34, [A first-person narrator who is also the protagonist is called],
  [a frame narrator],
  [a central narrator],
  [a peripheral narrator],
  [an omniscient narrator]
)

#mcq(35, [A peripheral first-person narrator is useful because],
  [he speaks in verse],
  [he knows everything],
  [he cannot be unreliable],
  [his limited position lets the hero remain partly mysterious]
)

#mcq(36, [The effect of a chapter that ends with an unanswered question is chiefly],
  [to make the reader carry the question into the next chapter],
  [to finish the story],
  [to introduce a new character],
  [to confuse the chronology]
)

#mcq(37, [Which is NOT a type of prose fiction?],
  [epistolary novel],
  [novella],
  [dumb show],
  [picaresque novel]
)

#mcq(38, [A novel's title most often functions as],
  [a first interpretation offered to the reader],
  [a dedication],
  [a label chosen by the publisher],
  [a summary of the plot]
)

#mcq(39, [“The narrator's sympathy for the villagers is clear from his choice of adjectives.” This is an argument about],
  [setting],
  [structure],
  [diction and its effect],
  [point of view alone]
)

#mcq(40, [The difference between story and plot matters most when a question asks],
  [what the setting is],
  [why the text begins where it does],
  [who the characters are],
  [what the theme is]
)

